# Each topic addition supplies three different questions, with concrete answer content.
def trio(sid,t,rows,mode='ea',note='',variant=''):
 for j,(p,a) in enumerate(rows):
  add(sid,f'{mode}-coverage-{t}-{variant}{j}',p,a,[('Accurate relevant concept or result',1),('Reasoning or application directly answers the question',1),('Necessary evidence, qualification or working',1)],**({'ut':[t]} if mode=='unit3' else {'et':[t]}),note=note)

def concept(sid,t,label,meaning,example,caution,mode='ea'):
 trio(sid,t,[('Explain '+label+'.',meaning),('Use the supplied example to explain '+label+':\n'+example[0],example[1]),('Evaluate this claim about '+label+': “'+caution[0]+'”',caution[1])],mode)
# Core science topics not exhausted by data-test skills.
concept('biology',0,'why species richness and evenness both matter when measuring biodiversity','Richness counts species; evenness describes how evenly individuals are distributed among them. Communities with the same richness can differ in evenness.',('Two sites each have four species. One has equal numbers of each; the other is dominated by one species.','Richness is equal, but the first site is more even. A diversity measure sensitive to abundance can distinguish them.'),('Counting species establishes every aspect of biodiversity.','The claim is too broad. Species richness does not describe abundance patterns, genetic diversity or ecosystem diversity.'))
concept('biology',4,'the difference between a habitat and an ecological niche','A habitat is where an organism lives. A niche includes its resource use, conditions and ecological relationships.',('Two bird species occupy the same forest but feed at different heights.','They share a broad habitat but differ in resource use and therefore aspects of their niches.'),('Species sharing a habitat must have identical niches.','False. They may use different food, locations or times, reducing direct competition.'))
concept('biology',7,'semi-conservative DNA replication','The two parental strands separate and serve as templates. Each resulting DNA molecule contains one parental strand and one newly synthesised complementary strand.',('One double-stranded molecule replicates once. Identify how parental strands are distributed.','Each daughter double helix receives one of the two parental strands and one new strand.'),('Both original strands stay together in one daughter molecule.','That describes a conservative arrangement, not semi-conservative replication. Each daughter molecule contains one original strand.'))
concept('biology',8,'how meiosis generates genetic variation','Crossing over between homologous chromosomes and independent assortment produce genetically varied gametes. Meiosis also reduces chromosome number.',('A diploid organism has 12 chromosomes in a somatic cell. Determine chromosome number in a normal gamete.','A gamete has 6 chromosomes. It receives one chromosome from each homologous pair after reduction division.'),('Meiosis produces genetically identical diploid cells.','False. It normally produces haploid products and crossing over and assortment generate variation; genetically identical diploid daughter cells are more characteristic of mitosis, subject to mutation.'))
concept('biology',9,'the relationship between transcription and translation','Transcription produces RNA from a DNA template. Translation uses mRNA codons at ribosomes to assemble amino acids into a polypeptide.',('An mRNA codon changes but the encoded amino acid stays the same. Explain why.','The genetic code is degenerate: more than one codon can specify the same amino acid. This can make a base substitution synonymous.'),('A ribosome reads DNA directly to make protein.','False. In cellular translation the ribosome reads mRNA; DNA is used as the transcription template.'))
concept('biology',10,'why mutations have different effects','A DNA sequence change may be synonymous, alter an amino acid, introduce a stop, shift a reading frame or affect regulation. Its effect depends on location and consequences for expression or function.',('One nucleotide is inserted into the middle of a protein-coding sequence. Explain a possible consequence.','A one-base insertion can shift the reading frame, changing downstream codons and potentially creating a premature stop. Effects depend on location and context.'),('Every mutation is harmful and changes a protein.','False. Some are neutral or beneficial; some do not change an amino acid, and regulatory or non-coding changes have varied effects.'))
concept('biology',12,'the different purposes of PCR and gel electrophoresis','PCR amplifies a targeted DNA region through cycles involving strand separation, primer annealing and extension. Gel electrophoresis separates DNA fragments, commonly by size.',('Two DNA fragments have the same charge-to-mass pattern but different lengths in an agarose gel. Predict their relative migration.','Under the usual conditions, the smaller fragment moves further through the gel toward the positive electrode in the same time.'),('PCR reveals a DNA sequence simply by making more copies.','Amplification alone does not read the sequence. Sequencing or an appropriate analytical method is needed to determine base order.'))
concept('biology',14,'the role of reproductive isolation in speciation','Reduced gene flow can permit populations to diverge through selection, drift and mutation. Reproductive barriers can maintain distinct gene pools.',('A geographic barrier separates a population for many generations. Does separation alone prove speciation?','No. It can reduce gene flow and permit divergence, but evidence of reproductive isolation or a justified species criterion is needed.'),('Similar appearance always proves close evolutionary relationship.','Convergent evolution can produce similar traits in different lineages. Multiple evidence sources, including homologous features and molecular data, are needed.'))
concept('chemistry',3,'Brønsted–Lowry acids and bases','An acid donates a proton and a base accepts one. Conjugate acid–base pairs differ by one proton.',('In NH₃ + H₂O ⇌ NH₄⁺ + OH⁻, identify the base and its conjugate acid.','NH₃ accepts a proton and is the base; NH₄⁺ is its conjugate acid. Water acts as the acid in this reaction.'),('A strong acid and a concentrated acid mean the same thing.','Strength concerns extent of ionisation; concentration concerns amount per volume. A strong acid can be dilute, and a weak acid can be concentrated.'))
concept('chemistry',4,'choosing a titration indicator','An appropriate indicator changes colour over a pH range within the steep change near equivalence for the particular titration.',('A strong-acid/strong-base titration has a steep pH change near equivalence. Explain what an indicator endpoint estimates.','The colour change estimates the point at which stoichiometric amounts have reacted. Endpoint and equivalence are related but are not identical concepts.'),('Every acid–base titration has equivalence at pH 7.','False. Equivalence pH depends on acid/base strength and the solution chemistry; weak-acid/strong-base titrations generally have basic equivalence conditions.'))
concept('chemistry',5,'how a weak-acid buffer resists pH change','A buffer contains a weak acid and its conjugate base in suitable amounts. The base reacts with added acid and the acid reacts with added base, limiting pH change within capacity.',('A small amount of H⁺ is added to a solution containing HA and A⁻. Explain the response.','A⁻ consumes much of the added H⁺ to form HA. The acid-to-base ratio changes, but the pH change is smaller than in an unbuffered solution under comparable conditions.'),('A buffer keeps pH exactly constant after any addition.','False. Buffers have finite capacity and pH changes as the component ratio changes. Large additions can overwhelm the buffer.'))
concept('chemistry',6,'oxidation and reduction using electron transfer','Oxidation is loss of electrons; reduction is gain of electrons. Oxidation number increases in oxidation and decreases in reduction.',('Zn(s) becomes Zn²⁺ while Cu²⁺ becomes Cu(s). Identify oxidation and reduction.','Zn loses two electrons and is oxidised. Cu²⁺ gains two electrons and is reduced.'),('The oxidising agent is the substance that loses electrons.','False. The oxidising agent causes oxidation of another species and is itself reduced by gaining electrons.'))
concept('chemistry',11,'how hydrogen bonding influences boiling point','Where molecules can form substantial intermolecular hydrogen bonding, stronger attractions generally require more energy to separate molecules, compared with otherwise comparable molecules.',('Compare ethanol with an ether of the same molecular formula. Explain why their boiling points may differ.','Ethanol has an O–H group and can hydrogen-bond strongly to other ethanol molecules. The ether lacks an O–H donor, so self-association differs; structure also matters.'),('Boiling breaks every covalent bond within a molecule.','False. Boiling primarily overcomes intermolecular attractions; intact molecules enter the gas phase under ordinary molecular boiling conditions.'))
concept('chemistry',12,'addition and substitution reactions','Addition joins atoms or groups across a multiple bond. Substitution replaces one atom or group with another while retaining the relevant skeleton.',('Ethene reacts with bromine to form a dibromoalkane. Classify the reaction.','It is addition: bromine adds across the C=C bond to form a saturated product with two C–Br bonds.'),('Every organic reaction converts an alkene into a polymer.','False. Organic reactions include addition, substitution, elimination, oxidation, reduction, condensation and others; conditions and functional groups determine pathways.'))
concept('chemistry',13,'addition polymerisation','Unsaturated monomers join to form long chains as their double bonds open. In the ideal addition process no small molecule is eliminated at each joining step.',('Identify the repeating unit from ethene.','Ethene gives a –CH₂–CH₂– repeating unit. Bonds connect many such units into polyethene.'),('All polymers biodegrade rapidly because they contain carbon.','False. Degradation depends on bond chemistry, structure and environment; carbon content alone establishes neither biodegradability nor rate.'))
concept('chemistry',14,'evaluating an organic synthesis route','A route can be compared using yield, selectivity, hazards, waste, energy and resource use. A high-yield step alone does not establish the best overall route.',('Route A has 90% yield but hazardous solvent and high energy demand; B has 80% yield with lower hazards. Identify a trade-off.','A produces more desired product per stated basis, while B may reduce hazards and energy demand. A recommendation needs explicit criteria and whole-route data.'),('The highest percentage yield always identifies the most sustainable route.','False. Yield does not alone measure atom economy, toxicity, solvent use, energy or waste across the route.'))
concept('chemistry',15,'using complementary analytical techniques','Infrared evidence can identify functional groups, mass spectrometry gives mass and fragmentation evidence, and NMR reveals chemical environments. Combining evidence strengthens structural identification.',('An infrared spectrum has a broad O–H feature but no molecular formula is supplied. Does it uniquely identify ethanol?','No. Many compounds contain O–H groups. Molecular formula and other spectral evidence are needed to distinguish structures.'),('One spectral peak always uniquely identifies a complete structure.','False. Peaks often support functional groups or fragments shared by many structures; an identification must fit all relevant evidence.'))
concept('physics',5,'the ideal transformer voltage–turns relationship','For an ideal transformer, V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p. A changing magnetic flux links the coils; ideal input and output powers are equal.',('An ideal transformer has 200 primary turns, 50 secondary turns and 240 V primary AC. Find secondary voltage.','V_s = 240 × 50/200 = 60 V AC. It is a step-down transformer.'),('An ordinary transformer operates continuously with steady DC in the same way as AC.','False. Steady DC does not provide the continuously changing flux required for normal transformer action.'))
concept('physics',6,'why simultaneity depends on reference frame','In special relativity, observers in relative motion may disagree about whether spatially separated events are simultaneous. The speed of light in vacuum is invariant in inertial frames.',('Two events occur at different places but are simultaneous in one inertial frame. Must every moving observer call them simultaneous?','No. Their time separation transforms with both relative velocity and spatial separation; simultaneity of separated events is frame-dependent.'),('Frame-dependent simultaneity means observations are arbitrary.','False. Lorentz transformations relate frame measurements precisely and preserve invariant physical relationships.'))
concept('physics',7,'time dilation and proper time','Proper time is measured between events by a clock present at both in its own rest frame. An inertial observer seeing that clock move measures a longer interval: Δt = γΔτ.',('A moving clock measures 4.0 s proper time and γ = 1.25. Find the other-frame interval.','Δt = γΔτ = 1.25 × 4.0 = 5.0 s.'),('An object is length-contracted equally in all directions.','False. In the standard result, length contraction occurs along relative motion; proper length is measured in the object’s rest frame.'))
concept('physics',8,'rest energy and relativistic total energy','Rest energy is E₀ = mc². Total energy is E = γmc², so relativistic kinetic energy is (γ − 1)mc².',('A particle has rest energy 10 units and γ = 1.5. Find total and kinetic energy.','Total energy = 15 units; kinetic energy = 15 − 10 = 5 units.'),('Relativistic momentum is always mv, even near light speed.','False. For a massive particle, p = γmv. The classical expression is an approximation at speeds much smaller than c.'))
concept('physics',10,'wave–particle duality','Quantum objects exhibit wave-like behaviour such as interference and particle-like discrete detection. The model used must fit the phenomenon being investigated.',('Electrons are detected as individual spots, but many spots form an interference pattern. Explain the evidence.','Discrete detections are particle-like events; the accumulated interference pattern demonstrates wave-like behaviour. Neither feature alone fully describes the quantum result.'),('Only photons have a de Broglie wavelength.','False. Matter has wavelength λ = h/p; the relation applies to momentum-bearing particles such as electrons.'))
concept('physics',11,'why Rutherford’s and Bohr’s atomic models differ','Rutherford’s nuclear model concentrates positive charge and mass in a small nucleus. Bohr adds quantised allowed electron energies to account for discrete spectra in a hydrogen-like model.',('Hydrogen emits specific wavelengths rather than a continuous spectrum. Explain the Bohr-model interpretation.','Transitions between discrete allowed energy levels emit photons with E = hf equal to the level-energy difference.'),('The Rutherford scattering experiment alone established every allowed electron energy.','False. Scattering supported a small dense nucleus; quantised electron energies required further modelling and spectroscopic evidence.'))
concept('physics',12,'the Standard Model distinction between quarks and leptons','Quarks and leptons are fundamental matter-particle families. Quarks participate in the strong interaction and combine into hadrons; leptons do not experience the strong interaction.',('A proton contains two up quarks of charge +2/3 each and one down quark of charge −1/3. Calculate its charge.','2(+2/3) + (−1/3) = +1 in units of the elementary charge.'),('A proton is a fundamental lepton.','False. A proton is a composite baryon made of quarks, not a lepton.'))
concept('psychology',5,'observational learning','People can acquire behaviour by observing models. Attention, retention, reproduction and motivation affect whether observed behaviour is later performed.',('A student watches a demonstration but cannot physically reproduce the skill. Explain one limit on learning performance.','Observation may support knowledge, but reproduction requires the capacity and practice to perform the action; observed behaviour is not automatically enacted.'),('People copy every behaviour they observe.','False. Attention, ability, perceived consequences and motivation influence whether a modelled behaviour is performed.'))
concept('psychology',7,'diffusion of responsibility in bystander behaviour','When several people are present, each may feel less personal responsibility to act. This can reduce helping under some conditions, but effects depend on context.',('A person directly asks one named bystander to call for help. Explain why this may change responsibility.','A direct individual request can make responsibility clear rather than leaving it shared ambiguously across the group.'),('A larger crowd always prevents helping.','False. Group size interacts with danger, social identity, clarity and other factors; “always” overstates a conditional tendency.'))
concept('psychology',8,'why aggression and attraction need multifactor explanations','Biological, cognitive, social and situational factors can influence aggression or attraction. A single correlate does not establish one universal cause.',('A survey finds that two behaviours occur together. Can it show one causes aggression?','Not by correlation alone. Direction, third variables, measurement and design must be considered before causal conclusions.'),('One biological measure fully explains every instance of aggression.','False. Behaviour reflects interacting influences and context; a single measure cannot support a universal explanation.'))
concept('psychology',9,'cognitive dissonance','Dissonance is discomfort associated with inconsistency among cognitions or between attitudes and behaviour. People may reduce it by changing behaviour, attitudes or interpretations.',('A person values sustainability but repeatedly buys disposable products, then argues that their purchases are too small to matter. Explain one interpretation.','The justification may reduce dissonance by minimising the behaviour’s significance rather than changing the behaviour. This is an interpretation, not proof of the person’s internal state.'),('Attitude and behaviour must always match.','False. Situational constraints, social pressures and competing motives can produce inconsistency; dissonance is one way to explain responses.'))
concept('psychology',10,'the fundamental attribution error','It is a tendency to overemphasise personal dispositions and underweight situational influences when explaining others’ behaviour, in relevant contexts.',('An observer calls a late student lazy without knowing the bus broke down. Explain the attribution problem.','The observer infers a disposition while overlooking a relevant situational cause. The bus information gives a concrete alternative explanation.'),('Every dispositional explanation is an attribution error.','False. Dispositions can matter. The error concerns an unjustified weighting of causes rather than any mention of personality.'))
concept('psychology',11,'the distinction between stereotypes, prejudice and discrimination','A stereotype is a generalised belief about a group; prejudice is an evaluative attitude; discrimination concerns behaviour or treatment. They are related but distinct.',('A manager assumes all older applicants lack technical skills and rejects one without assessing evidence. Identify belief and behaviour.','The group assumption is a stereotype; rejection based on it is discriminatory behaviour in the psychological sense. A legal finding would require the relevant legal test.'),('A stereotype and an individual’s observed ability are interchangeable evidence.','False. A group generalisation does not establish an individual’s characteristics and can distort fair assessment.'))
concept('psychology',12,'acculturation','Acculturation concerns cultural and psychological changes arising through contact between cultural groups. Experiences and strategies vary by individual and context.',('A young person retains family-language practices and also participates in the wider society. Explain a possible interpretation.','This can be understood as integration in a common acculturation framework: maintaining heritage connections alongside participation in the broader society. Do not assume the person’s identity without evidence.'),('Acculturation requires everyone to abandon their original culture.','False. Assimilation is only one possible pattern; maintaining heritage practices and combining affiliations are also possible.'))
concept('earth_science',4,'plate boundaries and geological hazards','Convergent, divergent and transform boundaries involve different relative motions and associated patterns of volcanism, earthquakes and deformation.',('Two plates move past one another along a major fault. Identify the boundary type and a likely hazard.','A transform boundary involves lateral motion. Earthquakes can occur as stress accumulates and is released along faults.'),('All plate boundaries generate identical volcanoes and earthquakes.','False. Boundary processes, crust type and geometry influence the hazard pattern.'))
concept('earth_science',5,'how an undersea earthquake can generate a tsunami','Rapid displacement of the seafloor can displace a large water volume, generating long waves that transform as water depth changes.',('A large earthquake occurs deep inland. Does its magnitude alone establish a tsunami?','No. Tsunami generation depends on displacement of water, source location, geometry and other factors, not earthquake magnitude alone.'),('Every undersea earthquake produces a damaging tsunami.','False. Sufficient water displacement and favourable source conditions are needed; size and coastal effects also vary.'))
concept('earth_science',6,'the difference between a hazard and its impacts','A hazard is a potentially damaging event or process. Impacts depend on exposure, vulnerability and capacity as well as event characteristics.',('Two places receive similar flood depths but have different building standards and warning systems. Explain why losses can differ.','Different vulnerability and preparedness can produce different damage and response despite similar physical exposure.'),('A severe natural event causes the same loss wherever it occurs.','False. Population, infrastructure, preparedness and social conditions mediate effects.'))
concept('earth_science',7,'human influence on hazard vulnerability','Land use, settlement, infrastructure and social conditions can increase or reduce vulnerability and exposure to hazards.',('New housing is built on a floodplain while emergency access remains limited. Identify two risk factors.','Floodplain settlement increases exposure; limited access can reduce evacuation and response capacity, increasing vulnerability.'),('Engineering protection removes all residual hazard risk.','False. Design limits, failure, maintenance and events beyond the design standard leave residual risk.'))
concept('earth_science',8,'greenhouse gases and radiative forcing','Greenhouse gases absorb and emit infrared radiation. Changes in their concentrations alter Earth’s energy balance, contributing to radiative forcing.',('Outgoing infrared radiation decreases initially after a greenhouse-gas increase, with incoming solar energy unchanged. Explain the imbalance.','Less energy initially escapes than enters, producing a positive energy imbalance until the climate system adjusts.'),('The greenhouse effect means no infrared energy escapes Earth.','False. Energy still escapes; greenhouse gases alter absorption, emission and the effective radiative balance rather than create a perfect barrier.'))
concept('earth_science',9,'distinguishing climate variability from a long-term trend','Variability concerns fluctuations over time; a trend is a sustained directional change. Causes can include internal variability and external forcings.',('One unusually cold season occurs within decades of rising global mean temperature. Does it disprove the trend?','No. A local or short-term fluctuation can occur within a long-term global trend; scale and duration must match the claim.'),('One weather observation establishes the global climate trend.','False. Long-term, spatially representative records and uncertainty analysis are needed.'))
concept('earth_science',10,'using proxy evidence to reconstruct past climate','Proxies such as ice cores, tree rings and sediments record properties related to climate. Calibration, dating and multiple records support interpretation.',('Two independently dated proxy records show a similar long-term change. Explain why agreement is useful.','Independent agreement can strengthen confidence in the inferred pattern, while differences in sensitivity, location and uncertainty still require evaluation.'),('A proxy is a perfect direct thermometer for all past climates.','False. Proxies require interpretation and calibration and have limits in resolution, sensitivity and dating.'))
concept('marine_science',4,'how marine protected areas can support conservation','Restrictions on extraction or damaging activities can reduce pressure, depending on design, compliance, connectivity and external threats.',('A protected reef still experiences a regional heatwave. Explain a limitation of local protection.','Local fishing restrictions may reduce one stressor but do not prevent regional ocean warming. Protection is not immunity from all pressures.'),('Declaring an area protected guarantees ecosystem recovery.','False. Enforcement, location, threat reduction and recovery time influence outcomes.'))
concept('marine_science',5,'links between ocean warming and reef stress','Higher temperatures can increase thermal stress and bleaching risk, while marine heatwaves and other environmental conditions affect severity.',('A heatwave coincides with bleaching at several reefs. What further evidence would strengthen a heat-stress explanation?','Temperature histories, species and depth controls, timing of bleaching and comparison sites help assess the link and competing stressors.'),('Every warm-water exposure produces the same bleaching response.','False. Duration, intensity, species, acclimatisation and local conditions affect response.'))
concept('marine_science',6,'why fish distribution changes','Temperature, habitat, food, recruitment, movement and fishing pressure can influence where fish occur and their abundance.',('Fish counts fall at one site but rise at a nearby site. Does this prove total population decline?','No. Redistribution or sampling differences are possible. Comparable surveys over the population’s range are needed.'),('A low catch always means the fish population is small.','False. Catch also depends on effort, gear, behaviour and conditions; use standardised measures and other evidence.'))
concept('marine_science',7,'bioaccumulation and biomagnification','Bioaccumulation is build-up of a substance within an organism over time. Biomagnification is an increase in concentration across higher trophic levels for suitable persistent substances.',('A persistent contaminant averages 1 unit in plankton, 5 in small fish and 20 in predators. Explain the pattern.','The increase across trophic levels is consistent with biomagnification. Feeding relationships, persistence and comparable concentration measures should be checked.'),('Every chemical biomagnifies in every food chain.','False. Chemical properties, elimination, metabolism and food-web structure determine whether magnification occurs.'))
concept('marine_science',9,'the distinction between maximum sustainable and economic fisheries yields','A biological yield target concerns sustained stock production; an economic target also accounts for revenue and harvesting costs. They need not coincide.',('Additional effort raises catch slightly but raises costs sharply. Explain why it may reduce economic return.','Marginal revenue may be below marginal cost, so profit can fall even while catch increases.'),('The largest possible catch is automatically the best long-term policy.','False. Stock sustainability, uncertainty, costs, ecosystem effects and management objectives must be considered.'))
concept('marine_science',10,'the role of monitoring and compliance in fisheries governance','Rules need credible monitoring, enforcement and review to influence behaviour and assess stock outcomes.',('A catch limit exists but landings are poorly recorded. Identify an evaluation problem.','Managers cannot reliably compare actual extraction with the limit or assess compliance; unreported removals can bias stock estimates.'),('A written rule is sufficient evidence that fishing pressure has fallen.','False. Implementation, actual removals, enforcement and independent monitoring are needed.'))
# Mathematics: extension beyond the Unit 3 question families.
trio('maths_general',6,[('A perpetuity pays $600 annually, first payment in one year. At 5% effective annual interest, find present value.','PV = payment/rate = 600/0.05 = $12,000. This assumes a constant rate and indefinite annual payments.'),('An ordinary annuity pays $1,000 at each year-end for 3 years. At 5% effective annual interest, calculate its present value.','PV = 1000[1 − (1.05)⁻³]/0.05 ≈ $2,723.25. Payments occur at the end of each year.'),('Explain why an annuity-due has a higher present value than an otherwise identical ordinary annuity at a positive interest rate.','Every annuity-due payment occurs one period earlier, so it is discounted for less time. Its present value is the ordinary-annuity present value multiplied by (1 + i).')])
trio('maths_general',7,[('An undirected simple graph has vertices A, B, C and edges AB and BC. Write its adjacency matrix in A, B, C order.','[[0,1,0],[1,0,1],[0,1,0]]. Each symmetric 1 records an edge and the diagonal is zero because there are no loops.'),('For adjacency matrix [[0,1,1],[1,0,0],[1,0,0]], determine each vertex degree.','Row sums are 2, 1 and 1 for this undirected simple graph; they give the degrees.'),('Explain what entry (A,C) of the square of an adjacency matrix counts.','It counts walks of length two from A to C, using the adjacency matrix conventions. Walks may revisit vertices; they are not necessarily simple paths.')])
trio('maths_general',8,[('A connected graph has vertex degrees 2, 2, 3, 3. Determine whether it has an Euler trail and an Euler circuit.','Exactly two odd-degree vertices permit an Euler trail beginning and ending at those vertices. It has no Euler circuit because not every degree is even.'),('Distinguish an Euler trail from a Hamiltonian path.','An Euler trail uses each edge exactly once. A Hamiltonian path visits each vertex exactly once; the two conditions concern different graph features.'),('A connected graph has all vertices of even degree. State the Eulerian conclusion and explain why it does not establish a Hamiltonian cycle.','It has an Euler circuit. The even-degree criterion concerns edge traversal, not visiting each vertex exactly once, so it does not prove a Hamiltonian cycle.')])
trio('maths_general',9,[('A triangle has edge weights AB = 2, BC = 3 and AC = 7. Find a minimum spanning tree and total weight.','Choose AB and BC. They connect all vertices without a cycle, with total weight 2 + 3 = 5.'),('Explain why a spanning tree on n vertices has n − 1 edges.','A tree is connected and cycle-free. Each added vertex can be connected by one edge, giving n − 1 edges; an extra edge would create a cycle.'),('Explain why choosing every smallest edge without checking for cycles is not a valid minimum-spanning-tree procedure.','A selection may create a cycle or fail to connect all vertices. A procedure such as Kruskal’s accepts low-weight edges only when they preserve acyclicity until all vertices connect.')])
trio('maths_general',11,[('A directed network has only two parallel source-to-sink routes: S–A–T with capacities 5 and 3, and S–B–T with capacities 4 and 6. Find maximum flow.','The first route carries at most 3 and the second at most 4. Maximum total flow is 7. The cut crossing A–T and S–B also has capacity 7.'),('Explain the relationship between maximum flow and minimum cut.','For a standard capacitated flow network, the greatest feasible source-to-sink flow equals the smallest source–sink cut capacity. A matching cut can justify optimality.'),('A cut has capacity 12 and a proposed feasible flow is 14. Identify the inconsistency.','No feasible source-to-sink flow can exceed any cut capacity. Either the flow violates constraints or the cut or capacity calculation is wrong.')])
trio('maths_general',12,[('Two workers A and B need two different jobs X and Y. Costs are A–X 4, A–Y 7, B–X 6, B–Y 3. Determine the minimum-cost assignment.','A to X and B to Y costs 4 + 3 = 7. The other feasible one-to-one assignment costs 7 + 6 = 13, so 7 is optimal.'),('Explain why choosing the cheapest worker for each job independently can fail in an assignment problem.','The same worker may be selected for more than one job when each worker can do only one. Feasible assignments must satisfy the one-to-one constraints.'),('If every entry in a row of a square assignment cost matrix is reduced by 5, explain why the optimal assignment is unchanged.','Every feasible complete assignment uses exactly one entry from that row, so every total falls by the same 5. Relative total costs remain unchanged.')])
trio('maths_methods',3,[('A rectangle has perimeter 20 m. Express area in terms of width x and determine the maximum area.','Length = 10 − x. A(x) = x(10 − x); A′ = 10 − 2x = 0 gives x = 5. A″ = −2 < 0, so maximum area is 25 m² for the valid domain 0 < x < 10.'),('Position is s(t) = t³ − 6t² + 9t metres. Find velocity and acceleration at t = 2 s.','v(t) = 3t² − 12t + 9; v(2) = −3 m s⁻¹. a(t) = 6t − 12; a(2) = 0 m s⁻².'),('Explain why velocity equal to zero does not necessarily imply acceleration equal to zero.','Velocity is the first derivative of position and acceleration is its rate of change. At a turning point an object can have instantaneous zero velocity while its velocity is changing.')])
trio('maths_methods',6,[('Evaluate ∫₀² 3x² dx.','An antiderivative is x³. Evaluate [x³]₀² = 8 − 0 = 8.'),('Find the geometric area between y = x and the x-axis on −1 ≤ x ≤ 1.','Split at zero: area = −∫₋₁⁰ x dx + ∫₀¹ x dx = 1/2 + 1/2 = 1 square unit. The signed integral across the full interval is zero.'),('Explain why a definite integral can be zero even when the graph encloses non-zero area.','A definite integral is signed; positive and negative contributions can cancel. Geometric area requires summing non-negative areas, often by splitting at intercepts.')])
trio('maths_methods',7,[('Two triangle sides are 3 cm and 4 cm with included angle 90°. Use the cosine rule to find the opposite side.','c² = 3² + 4² − 2(3)(4)cos 90° = 25, so c = 5 cm.'),('In a triangle, a = 10 cm, A = 30° and B = 60°. Use the sine rule to find b.','b/sin 60° = 10/sin 30°. Thus b = 10√3 ≈ 17.32 cm.'),('Explain why an inverse-sine calculation can produce an ambiguous triangle case.','Sine has the same value for supplementary angles. With certain side-side-angle data, two angles can satisfy the ratio; remaining triangle conditions must be checked.')])
trio('maths_methods',8,[('A continuous variable has density f(x) = 1/4 for 0 ≤ x ≤ 4 and zero elsewhere. Calculate P(1 < X < 3).','Probability is density area: (3 − 1) × 1/4 = 1/2.'),('For the same uniform density on [0,4], calculate E(X) and P(X = 2).','E(X) = (0 + 4)/2 = 2. For a continuous variable, the probability at one exact point is zero.'),('Explain why a probability density may be greater than 1 without being invalid.','Probability is area, not height alone. A density is valid when it is non-negative and its total integral is 1; a narrow interval can have height above 1.')])
trio('maths_methods',10,[('A random sample of 200 voters contains 116 supporters. Calculate the sample proportion.','p̂ = 116/200 = 0.58. This estimates a population proportion; it is not proof that exactly 58% of the population supports the proposal.'),('Explain one advantage of a random sample over a voluntary online poll for estimating a population proportion.','Random sampling gives defined selection probabilities and can reduce self-selection bias. Non-response and coverage still require attention.'),('An online survey has 20,000 self-selected responses. Does its size guarantee an unbiased population estimate?','No. A large sample can still have systematic selection or coverage bias. Size reduces sampling variability under appropriate conditions, not all bias.')])
trio('maths_specialist',4,[('A line passes through (1,2,3) in direction (2,−1,4). Write a vector equation.','r = (1,2,3) + t(2,−1,4), t ∈ ℝ. At t = 0 it passes through the point and its direction is the stated vector.'),('A plane has normal (1,2,−1) and passes through (2,0,1). Find its Cartesian equation.','(1,2,−1) · ((x,y,z) − (2,0,1)) = 0. Thus x + 2y − z = 1.'),('Explain how to determine whether a line is parallel to a plane from direction vector d and plane normal n.','Compute d · n. A zero result means the line’s direction is parallel to the plane; check a point to distinguish a contained line from a separate parallel line.')])
trio('maths_specialist',7,[('Evaluate ∫2x cos(x²) dx.','Let u = x², so du = 2x dx. The integral becomes ∫cos u du = sin u + C = sin(x²) + C.'),('Evaluate ∫x eˣ dx by parts.','Choose u = x and dv = eˣ dx. Then du = dx, v = eˣ. Integral = xeˣ − ∫eˣ dx = xeˣ − eˣ + C.'),('Explain why an indefinite integral requires an arbitrary constant.','All functions differing by a constant have the same derivative. The constant represents the family of antiderivatives unless an initial condition fixes it.')])
trio('maths_specialist',8,[('Find the volume when y = x on 0 ≤ x ≤ 2 is rotated about the x-axis.','V = π∫₀² x² dx = π[x³/3]₀² = 8π/3 cubic units.'),('A solid has circular cross-sections of radius r(x) = 2 on 0 ≤ x ≤ 3. Find its volume by integration.','V = ∫₀³ π(2)² dx = 4π × 3 = 12π cubic units.'),('Explain why integrating a radius directly does not give a solid’s volume.','Volume is the integral of cross-sectional area along an axis. For disks, area is πr²; integrating radius alone omits the required geometric area factor.')])
trio('maths_specialist',10,[('A particle starts at x(0) = 2 and has velocity v(t) = 3t². Determine x(t).','Integrate: x(t) = t³ + C. The initial condition gives C = 2, so x(t) = t³ + 2.'),('A particle has velocity v(t) = t − 1 for 0 ≤ t ≤ 2. Find displacement and total distance.','Displacement = ∫₀²(t − 1)dt = 0. Split at t = 1: distance = ∫₀¹(1 − t)dt + ∫₁²(t − 1)dt = 1 unit.'),('Explain why displacement and distance can differ for one-dimensional motion.','Displacement integrates signed velocity, whereas distance integrates speed |v|. Reversing direction can cancel displacement but still adds distance.')])
trio('maths_specialist',11,[('A population has mean 50 and standard deviation 12. For independent samples of size 36, state the mean and standard deviation of the sample mean.','E(X̄) = 50. SD(X̄) = σ/√n = 12/6 = 2, assuming independent sampling with appropriate conditions.'),('Known population σ = 10, n = 100 and sample mean 40. Calculate an approximate 95% normal confidence interval for the population mean.','SE = 10/√100 = 1. Margin = 1.96. Interval = 40 ± 1.96 = [38.04, 41.96], under the stated normal-interval conditions.'),('Interpret 95% confidence using repeated sampling.','About 95% of intervals constructed by the same valid procedure over repeated samples would contain the fixed population mean. It is not a statement that 95% of observations lie in this interval.')])
