# Additional breadth where a topic previously had only one question.
concept('biology',11,'predicting a monohybrid cross under simple dominance','For heterozygotes Aa × Aa with complete dominance, genotype proportions are 1 AA:2 Aa:1 aa, and expected phenotype proportions are 3 dominant:1 recessive.',('Two Aa parents have one child. Calculate the model probability of genotype aa.','Each parent transmits a with probability 1/2; independent transmission gives 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4. This is a probability for each conception, not a guaranteed family ratio.'),('A dominant allele must be the most common allele in a population.','False. Dominance describes expression in a heterozygote, not population frequency.'))
concept('biology',13,'natural selection compared with genetic drift','Selection changes allele frequencies through differences in reproductive success associated with heritable variation. Drift is random frequency change due to finite population sampling and can be stronger in small populations.',('A storm leaves a small random subset of a population alive, changing allele frequencies. Explain a possible process.','A bottleneck can produce drift when survivors are a non-representative random sample. Do not assume every frequency change reflects adaptive advantage.'),('Individual organisms evolve new useful alleles because they need them.','False. Populations evolve through changes in heritable variation over generations; mutation is not directed by an organism’s need.'))
concept('chemistry',9,'functional groups and organic classification','Functional groups provide characteristic structural and reaction features. Alcohols contain hydroxyl groups, carboxylic acids contain carboxyl groups and esters contain an ester linkage.',('Classify CH₃CH₂OH and state its systematic name.','It is an alcohol, ethanol. The two-carbon chain and hydroxyl group determine the name under the stated simple structure.'),('All compounds containing oxygen are alcohols.','False. Oxygen occurs in several functional groups, including carbonyls, ethers, acids and esters.'))
concept('chemistry',10,'structural and stereoisomerism','Structural isomers share a molecular formula but differ in connectivity. Stereoisomers share connectivity but differ in spatial arrangement.',('Butan-1-ol and butan-2-ol have the same molecular formula. Explain their isomer relationship.','They are structural positional isomers because the hydroxyl group is attached at a different position on the carbon skeleton.'),('Any bond rotation changes one compound into a different structural isomer.','False. Rotation around a single bond changes conformation without changing connectivity.'))
concept('earth_science',11,'the role of models in climate projections','Climate models represent interacting physical processes. Projections depend on scenarios, model structure and variability and should communicate uncertainty.',('Two emissions scenarios produce different later-century projections. Does this alone show a model error?','No. Different forcing assumptions can produce different plausible outcomes. Compare assumptions and uncertainty before judging performance.'),('Uncertainty means a model contains no useful information.','False. A model can constrain plausible outcomes and test mechanisms while retaining quantified uncertainty.'))
concept('psychology',6,'conformity and social influence','Conformity involves changes in behaviour or belief in relation to group pressure. Normative influence concerns acceptance; informational influence concerns using others as evidence.',('A student privately disagrees but publicly follows the group to avoid rejection. Identify a plausible influence.','Normative influence is plausible because acceptance and rejection, rather than private belief change, are central to the scenario.'),('Public agreement always proves private acceptance.','False. Compliance can occur without internalisation; the evidence must distinguish behaviour from belief.'))
concept('economics',8,'interest-rate transmission through consumption','Higher borrowing rates can raise repayments and discourage new borrowing, reducing some households’ spending. Savings income and other channels can create varied effects.',('A borrower has a fixed-rate loan that has not yet reset. Explain one reason policy effects can be delayed.','Current repayments may initially remain unchanged, delaying that cash-flow channel; expectations and other channels may still operate.'),('Every household reduces spending by exactly the same amount after a rate rise.','False. Debt, savings, loan contracts, income and expectations differ.'))
concept('economics',9,'supply-side policy and productive capacity','Policies that improve skills, competition, infrastructure or incentives can affect productive capacity and efficiency, often with lags and distributional trade-offs.',('A training program addresses a documented shortage of qualified workers. Explain one potential benefit and limitation.','It may improve labour suitability and productive capacity, but training takes time and must match actual demand; completion does not guarantee employment.'),('Supply-side reform eliminates every macroeconomic trade-off immediately.','False. Implementation, adjustment costs, lags and demand conditions affect outcomes.'))
concept('economics',10,'policy lags and uncertainty','Recognition, decision, implementation and response can take time. Economic relationships and external conditions can change before effects are realised.',('A stimulus project begins after private demand has already recovered. Identify a timing concern.','It may add demand at a different phase than intended, potentially increasing inflationary pressure or competing for resources.'),('A policy’s announced intention proves its eventual effect.','False. Evaluation needs implementation and outcome evidence, a causal explanation and relevant limits.'))
concept('economics',11,'criterion-based policy evaluation','Compare alternatives using explicit economic criteria, relevant evidence and causal reasoning. Consider time horizons, trade-offs and uncertainty.',('A policy raises GDP but worsens a distributional outcome. Explain why the evaluation depends on criteria.','A growth criterion may favour it while equity raises concerns. A justified judgement explains weighting and whether complementary measures can address costs.'),('A recommendation is justified merely by saying a policy is effective.','False. Specify effective for which objective, through what mechanism and based on what evidence.'))
concept('food',4,'food allergy and intolerance as distinct design considerations','Allergy involves an immune response; intolerance has different mechanisms and is not simply another word for allergy. Ingredient identification and cross-contact control can be critical to a defined allergen brief.',('A brief requires a dairy-free product, but the factory uses shared equipment without an assessed cleaning process. Identify a design and production concern.','Removing dairy from the recipe does not alone address cross-contact. The process needs appropriate controls and truthful information against the stated brief.'),('A vegan label automatically guarantees freedom from every food allergen.','False. Vegan describes ingredient origins, not absence of allergens such as nuts, soy or wheat or all cross-contact.'))
concept('food',4,'mandatory constraints compared with desirable features in a food brief','Mandatory constraints are requirements a solution must satisfy; desirable criteria distinguish acceptable options. Evaluate both explicitly.',('A sauce tastes good but exceeds the brief’s maximum cost. Evaluate it.','It fails a mandatory cost constraint if the maximum is fixed. Reformulation must preserve required function and acceptability while meeting cost.'),('A high score on flavour always compensates for a failed safety requirement.','False. Safety and mandatory constraints cannot simply be averaged away by strengths elsewhere.'),'unit3')
concept('marine_science',8,'mark–recapture population estimation','A simple estimate is N ≈ MC/R, with assumptions about population closure, mixing, tag retention and comparable capture probability.',('M=60 fish are marked; a later sample of C=80 includes R=12 marked fish. Estimate population size.','N ≈ 60 × 80/12 = 400 fish. It is an estimate contingent on the sampling assumptions.'),('Tag loss has no effect on a mark–recapture estimate.','False. Tag loss can reduce observed recaptures and inflate the simple estimate; it violates a key assumption.'))
concept('maths_general',0,'association without causal proof','Association describes a relationship between variables. Observational association can arise from causation, confounding, reverse direction or other patterns.',('Ice-cream sales and swimming incidents both rise in summer. Explain a plausible confounder.','Hot weather or season can increase both ice-cream purchases and exposure to swimming. The association does not establish that ice cream causes incidents.'),('A strong correlation proves that x causes y.','False. Strength of association does not establish causal direction or remove confounding.'))
# The same foundational association questions also fit Unit 3 bivariate analysis.
for q in Q:
 if q['subject']=='maths_general' and '-coverage-0-' in q['id']:q['scopes']['unit3']={'route':'maths_general','topics':[U['maths_general']['topics'][0]]}
trio('maths_general',5,[('An investment of $2,000 earns 5% annually for two years with no transactions. Calculate its balance.','A = 2000(1.05)² = $2,205. Interest earned = $205.'),('A loan has opening balance $1,000. One month’s interest is 1%, followed by a $100 repayment. Calculate closing balance.','Interest = $10; closing balance = $1,000 + $10 − $100 = $910. The stated order of interest then payment matters.'),('Explain why a loan balance may fall slowly at the start of a reducing-balance repayment plan.','Interest is calculated on a relatively large balance, so more of each payment initially services interest. As principal falls, less interest is charged, under otherwise fixed conditions.')])
trio('maths_general',10,[('Activities A=2 days and B=5 days start together. C=4 days follows A. D=3 days follows both B and C. Determine project duration and critical path.','A finishes day 2; C finishes day 6; B finishes day 5. D starts day 6 and ends day 9. Critical path is A–C–D, total 9 days.'),('In the same defined network A=2, B=5, C=4 after A, D=3 after B and C, determine B’s float.','D cannot start before day 6, so B can finish at day 6 rather than day 5. B has 1 day of float.'),('Explain why shortening a non-critical activity may not shorten the project.','If it already finishes before the controlling predecessor chain, reducing its duration does not advance the earliest finish. The critical path and possible changes in it must be checked.')])
trio('maths_methods',9,[('X has a normal distribution with mean 100 and SD 15. Find the z-score for 130.','z = (130 − 100)/15 = 2. It is two standard deviations above the mean.'),('For standard normal Z, Φ(1)=0.8413. Calculate P(Z>1).','P(Z>1) = 1 − 0.8413 = 0.1587.'),('Explain why a normal model cannot be accepted merely because a variable has a mean and standard deviation.','Many distributions have those summaries. Shape, context, support and appropriate goodness-of-fit evidence are needed to justify normality.')])
trio('maths_methods',11,[('Using p̂=0.5, n=100 and z=1.96, calculate the approximate margin of error for a 95% proportion interval.','SE = √(0.5×0.5/100)=0.05. Margin = 1.96×0.05=0.098, or 9.8 percentage points.'),('With p̂ held constant, explain the effect of quadrupling sample size on the standard error of a proportion.','SE = √[p̂(1−p̂)/n]. Quadrupling n divides SE by 2, so the margin of error is halved at the same confidence level.'),('Explain why a confidence interval does not automatically correct a biased sampling method.','The interval quantifies sampling uncertainty under assumptions. Systematic selection or coverage bias can shift estimates without being captured by the calculated margin.')])
trio('maths_specialist',2,[('Prove by induction that 1+3+…+(2n−1)=n² for positive integer n.','Base n=1: 1=1². Assume sum to k is k². Add next odd term 2k+1: k²+2k+1=(k+1)². Thus the result follows for every positive integer n.'),('Explain why a base case and an inductive implication are both needed.','The base anchors the chain at the first valid integer; the implication carries truth from each integer to the next. Neither alone establishes the entire stated range.'),('A proof checks n=1,2,3 and concludes a formula holds for all positive integers. Evaluate it.','Examples can suggest a pattern but do not prove it for infinitely many integers. A valid induction or another general argument is required.')])
for q in Q:
 if q['subject']=='maths_specialist' and '-coverage-2-' in q['id']:q['scopes']['unit3']={'route':'maths_specialist','topics':[U['maths_specialist']['topics'][1]]}
trio('maths_specialist',9,[('Solve dy/dx=3y with y(0)=2.','Separate dy/y=3dx, integrate ln|y|=3x+C, so y=Ae³ˣ. The initial condition gives A=2; y=2e³ˣ.'),('For dy/dt=−0.2y with y(0)=100, find y at t=5.','y=100e⁻⁰⋅²ᵗ, so y(5)=100/e≈36.79. This is decay in the stipulated model.'),('Explain why an initial condition is needed for a unique member of a differential-equation solution family.','Integration introduces an arbitrary constant. A compatible initial value fixes that constant under the relevant existence and uniqueness conditions.')])
concept('physics',9,'the photoelectric threshold and photon energy','Photon energy is hf. Electrons are emitted only if photon energy is sufficient to overcome the material’s work function; maximum kinetic energy is hf−φ in the simple model.',('Photon energy is 5 eV and work function 2 eV. Find maximum emitted-electron kinetic energy.','Kmax=5−2=3 eV. The excess energy above the work function appears as maximum kinetic energy.'),('Doubling intensity at fixed frequency always doubles maximum electron kinetic energy.','False. Above threshold, higher intensity mainly changes the photon arrival rate and potential emitted-electron count; Kmax depends on frequency and work function.'))
trio('legal',3,[('Explain why the validity of each law matters before applying constitutional inconsistency to a Commonwealth–state conflict.','The analysis first asks whether each parliament acted within its constitutional power. The inconsistency rule is not a general way to rescue an otherwise invalid Commonwealth law.'),('A state law conflicts with only one separable part of a valid Commonwealth scheme. Explain why saying the entire state Act is abolished is inaccurate.','The constitutional consequence operates to the extent of inconsistency. Its scope depends on the conflict; the whole Act is not automatically removed.'),('Explain why federalism involves both divided power and potential overlap.','Some powers are exclusive, some concurrent and some remain with states. Overlap can produce coordinated regulation or conflict requiring constitutional resolution.')],'unit3')
trio('legal',6,[('Identify one scrutiny function that a Queensland parliamentary committee can perform on a bill.','It can examine provisions, seek submissions and report on issues to inform the Legislative Assembly. It does not itself give royal assent or replace parliamentary passage.'),('Explain one potential benefit and one limitation of a single legislative chamber.','A single chamber can streamline passage but removes a second chamber of review. Evaluation should consider committees, public scrutiny and other accountability measures.'),('A Queensland bill passes the Legislative Assembly. Must it then pass a Queensland Senate? Explain.','No. Queensland has no upper house or Senate. The remaining legislative process includes assent and applicable commencement arrangements.')],'unit3')
trio('legal',7,[('Explain why a High Court ruling on constitutional validity differs from parliament deciding whether a policy is desirable.','Constitutional review concerns legal authority and limits. A policy can be politically attractive yet outside power, or valid despite political disagreement.'),('A court rejects a constitutional challenge to a law. Does this prove the law is the fairest available policy? Explain.','No. Legal validity and policy evaluation use different questions. Fairness, effectiveness and alternatives can still be debated.'),('Explain why constitutional interpretation can have consequences beyond the parties to a case.','Authoritative legal principles guide later cases and government action. An interpretation can clarify the reach of institutions’ powers and constrain future decisions.')],'unit3')
trio('legal',9,[('Distinguish a specialist tribunal’s subject-matter expertise from unlimited jurisdiction.','Expertise concerns knowledge and procedures; jurisdiction is the legal authority to hear particular matters and grant particular remedies. Expertise does not create unlimited powers.'),('A fictional tribunal is inexpensive but its hearing venue is inaccessible to some users. Evaluate one access issue.','Low fees improve financial access, but physical or communication barriers can still prevent effective participation. Accessible venues or arrangements should be considered.'),('Explain why review options matter when evaluating a specialist dispute body.','Review can correct legal or other permitted errors and support accountability. The available route and scope depend on governing law and should not be assumed.')],'unit3')
# Human-rights topic questions remain separate from governance practice.
legal_extra={
0:[('A treaty creates international obligations for its states parties. Explain why that does not mean every UN member is bound by that treaty.','Treaty participation and consent matter. UN membership is not identical to becoming a party to each separate human-rights treaty.'),('Explain why a treaty monitoring report and an Australian court order have different legal roles.','Monitoring evaluates international compliance and may issue findings or recommendations. A domestic court order arises from a court’s jurisdiction under applicable law; they are not interchangeable.')],
1:[('Distinguish equality in formal rules from substantive equality.','Formal equality applies the same legal rule; substantive equality examines practical disadvantage and whether different measures are needed for meaningful equal enjoyment.'),('A person has a right on paper but cannot understand the complaint process. Explain the implementation gap.','Recognition does not guarantee access to a remedy. Language, cost, information and assistance can determine whether the right is practically usable.')],
3:[('Explain why refugee protection requires an individual’s circumstances to be examined.','The refugee definition depends on a well-founded fear of persecution for a Convention reason and other relevant criteria. Nationality alone does not decide every claim.'),('Explain why the Refugee Convention and its Protocol should not be described as Australian statutes.','They are international instruments. Domestic implementation and decision-making operate through Australian law and relevant legal processes.')],
4:[('A public service uses a rule that appears neutral but disproportionately excludes a racial group. Identify the legal issue that requires investigation.','Possible indirect racial discrimination requires examination under the applicable legislation, including disadvantage and the relevant reasonableness or justification test.'),('Explain why a claim of racial discrimination needs evidence of treatment and the applicable legal test.','A label alone does not establish a breach. Identify the conduct, protected basis, context, disadvantage and any relevant exceptions or justification.')],
5:[('Explain why formal legal equality may not eliminate discrimination against women in practice.','Social barriers, unequal resources and institutional practices may persist despite equal wording. Evaluate actual access, treatment and outcomes with evidence.'),('A reform announces equality but provides no enforcement mechanism. Identify one limitation.','Affected people may lack a practical avenue to challenge breaches or obtain remedies. Implementation, authority and accessible processes matter.')],
6:[('A service is available to everyone but provides information only in an inaccessible format. Explain a disability-rights concern.','Formal availability may not provide equal practical access. The relevant legal duties and reasonable adjustments or accessibility requirements must be examined.'),('Explain why consulting people with disability can improve a rights reform.','Their participation can reveal barriers and inform workable measures. Consultation should influence decisions and respect diverse needs, rather than assume one person represents all.')],
7:[('Explain why the prohibition on torture should not be evaluated only through claimed administrative convenience.','Convenience does not remove the international prohibition or justify torture. A legal evaluation must recognise the nature of the obligation and distinguish lawful alternatives.'),('Distinguish investigating an allegation of torture from assuming it has been legally established.','An allegation requires appropriate investigation and evidence under relevant processes. Taking it seriously does not mean treating untested assertions as proven findings.')],
8:[('Explain why considering children’s views differs from making children solely responsible for a decision.','Participation recognises children as rights holders, while decision-makers retain responsibilities appropriate to age, maturity, protection and the governing framework.'),('A policy affects children but gathers only adult feedback. Identify one limitation in a rights-based analysis.','It may overlook children’s experiences and perspectives. Age-appropriate, safe participation can improve evidence while respecting protection requirements.')],
10:[('Explain why a state’s acceptance of an international obligation matters in evaluating a sovereignty objection.','Sovereignty includes a state’s capacity to undertake international obligations. A later claim of domestic discretion must be examined against commitments and applicable law.'),('Distinguish international criticism from automatic international enforcement inside Australia.','Criticism and monitoring can influence conduct, but enforcement depends on powers, consent, institutions and domestic legal arrangements; there is no universal automatic enforcement mechanism.')],
11:[('Explain one difference between a UN treaty body and the UNHCR.','Treaty bodies monitor particular treaty obligations. UNHCR has an international refugee-protection mandate; its role is not identical to every treaty committee.'),('A source calls the UN a single court that hears every rights complaint. Correct the claim.','The UN has multiple organs, agencies and treaty mechanisms with different mandates. Jurisdiction, admissibility and state participation limit particular complaint processes.')],
12:[('Explain why the historical influence of Magna Carta should be distinguished from its original scope.','Later constitutional ideas drew on limits to arbitrary power, but the original medieval document did not grant a modern universal set of rights to all people.'),('A response calls Magna Carta the same instrument as the UDHR. Identify two errors.','They arose in different historical settings and have different forms and purposes. Magna Carta is a medieval charter; the UDHR is a modern international declaration.')],
13:[('Distinguish identifying potentially discriminatory conduct from selecting the correct complaint authority.','First identify the conduct and legal ground; then determine which jurisdiction and legislation apply. Different bodies have different powers and procedures.'),('Explain why a neutral-sounding rule can still require discrimination analysis.','Its operation may disadvantage a protected group. The applicable legal test examines effects and relevant justification, not only whether the words mention the group.')],
14:[('Explain why a constitutional limit on governmental power and a statutory complaint right should not be treated as identical.','They have different legal sources, operation, scope and remedies. A constitutional argument may challenge validity, while a statutory process depends on legislative rights and procedures.'),('A source says every UDHR right is expressly written into the Australian Constitution. Evaluate it.','The claim is incorrect. The Constitution contains some express protections and implications but not a comprehensive incorporation of all UDHR rights.')],
15:[('Explain why political communication and all personal speech are different categories in constitutional analysis.','The implied freedom concerns communication relevant to representative and responsible government. It does not create an unlimited general personal freedom covering every utterance.'),('A protest restriction is said to be reasonable because some people dislike the message. Identify a weakness in that reasoning.','Dislike alone does not establish lawful justification. Analyse the relevant law, purpose, effects and applicable constitutional or statutory limits.')],
16:[('Explain how interpreter access can affect fairness in a legal proceeding.','Without understanding and communication support, a person may struggle to participate, instruct representatives or respond to evidence. The relevant procedural safeguards depend on the proceeding.'),('Distinguish an acquittal from proof that no rights concern could have occurred during a case.','An acquittal resolves the charge under the criminal process; it does not automatically determine every separate issue about treatment, procedure or remedies.')],
17:[('Explain one potential advantage and one limitation of conciliation.','It can offer flexible, less adversarial resolution, but depends on participation and agreement. It does not necessarily produce a determination of legal liability.'),('A complaint remains unresolved after conciliation. Explain why the next step must be checked against the governing law.','Time limits, jurisdiction and eligibility determine further review or court options. There is no single automatic next step for every complaint type.')],
18:[('Distinguish the Commission’s education function from its complaint-handling role.','Education promotes understanding and prevention. Complaint handling addresses particular matters within statutory powers; education materials are not themselves binding court orders.'),('Explain why the Commission cannot simply rewrite an Act to resolve a complaint.','Legislative change belongs to parliament. The Commission’s powers include specified inquiry, conciliation and advisory functions, not a general power to legislate.')],
19:[('Explain how courts and parliament can contribute differently to rights reform.','Courts interpret and apply law within jurisdiction, which can expose limits or gaps. Parliament can change legislation through the legislative process; institutional roles differ.'),('A law reform body recommends a new complaint process. Explain what evidence would establish actual implementation.','Look for enacted and commenced legislation, operational authority, procedures and resources. The recommendation alone is not implementation.')],
20:[('Explain one way a rights advocacy group can influence reform without making law itself.','It can gather evidence, make submissions, campaign or support affected people, influencing public and parliamentary debate. Parliament or another authorised body makes the relevant legal change.'),('A campaign receives many social-media shares. Does that establish legal reform or improved rights protection? Explain.','No. Shares show reach or engagement, not enactment, implementation or outcomes. Examine the responsible authority’s action and practical effects.')],
21:[('A fictional rights proposal reduces application fees but keeps forms inaccessible. Analyse one consequence for people with limited English.','Financial access may improve while language barriers remain, so some affected people still cannot use the process effectively. A complete analysis distinguishes groups and barriers.'),('A fictional reform debate includes only the government’s view. Identify evidence needed for analysis.','Seek affected people’s experiences, decision-makers’ reasons, service data and alternative viewpoints. Compare consequences rather than assuming one source represents every stakeholder.')],
22:[('A fictional rights reform improves access but requires substantial funding. Explain how to make a justified recommendation.','Apply explicit legal criteria, compare alternatives using evidence, weigh access benefits against implementation costs and identify implications and the responsible authority.'),('Explain why naming fairness without applying it does not complete evaluation.','The response must explain whose interests are affected, what fair treatment requires in context and how the evidence supports a judgement between alternatives.')]
}
for t,rows in legal_extra.items():
 for j,(p,a) in enumerate(rows):add('legal',f'ea-breadth-{t}-{j}',p,a,[('Accurate relevant legal concept',1),('Direct application or distinction',1),('Reasoned explanation with appropriate limits',1)],et=[t],note='Original legal practice. Explanations are summaries; fictional proposals are not descriptions of current law.',sources=['https://www.ohchr.org/en/instruments-and-mechanisms/international-human-rights-law','https://humanrights.gov.au/complaints'])
concept('economics',4,'distinguishing a trend from a single change in trade data','A trend is a sustained pattern across multiple observations. A single increase or decrease does not establish its persistence or cause.',('Exports rise in one quarter after three declining quarters. What can be concluded?','The latest quarter increased, but a sustained reversal is not established. Compare a longer series, seasonality, prices and quantities.'),('A dollar-value rise proves export volume increased.','False. Export value reflects both price and quantity; separate measures are needed.'),'unit3')
concept('atsi_studies',0,'using historical context to interpret rights and freedoms','Identify the relevant time, jurisdiction, policy and affected peoples. Rights and restrictions changed unevenly, so avoid one universal timeline without evidence.',('A source describes a restriction under one state policy in one decade. Can it establish identical conditions everywhere?','No. Investigate other jurisdictions, periods and communities. The source can support a bounded claim about its own context.'),('The 1967 referendum immediately ended every discriminatory policy.','False. Its constitutional changes did not automatically remove all legislation, institutional practices or inequality.'),'unit3')
concept('atsi_studies',1,'interpreting responses to restrictive government policies','Examine advocacy, resistance, adaptation and diverse experiences using sources with clear provenance. A policy’s official purpose is not the same as its effects.',('An administrative report describes a policy as beneficial while affected people describe harm. Explain an analytical task.','Compare purpose, perspective and evidence of effects. Do not treat the official label as proof of lived benefit or erase the affected people’s accounts.'),('One official document establishes all community responses.','False. Seek community voices and diverse evidence while respecting authority, permissions and context.'),'unit3')
concept('atsi_studies',0,'recognition and reconciliation as related but different concepts','Recognition acknowledges peoples, histories or status. Reconciliation also involves relationships, justice, respect and practical institutional change; recognition alone does not prove all outcomes.',('A public ceremony acknowledges history but service barriers remain unchanged. Explain the distinction.','The ceremony may contribute to recognition while practical barriers still need action. Evaluate its purpose and limits rather than treating it as complete reconciliation.'),('One commemorative event proves reconciliation is complete.','False. Examine sustained relationships, justice, institutional action and community-defined outcomes.'))
concept('atsi_studies',2,'linking historical policy to contemporary reconciliation','Analysis should identify a specific policy, its effects and the evidence connecting later responses. Avoid assuming either that past effects have disappeared or that every current outcome has one cause.',('An organisation apologises for a past role but provides no support for affected people. Identify an evaluation issue.','Acknowledgement and practical redress are distinct. Assess commitments, action and affected people’s perspectives rather than apology alone.'),('A historical policy’s official intention fully describes its consequences.','False. Evaluate actual effects, implementation and lived experience with appropriate sources.'))
concept('accounting',1,'the relationship between journals and ledgers','The journal records transaction entries; the ledger groups postings by account. Debits and credits preserve the accounting structure when correctly applied.',('A business receives $1,000 cash from a customer paying an existing receivable. State the entry.','Debit cash $1,000 and credit accounts receivable $1,000. This collection does not create new sales revenue.'),('Every debit decreases an account balance.','False. Effects depend on account type; debits normally increase assets and expenses and reduce liabilities, equity or income balances under standard conventions.'))
concept('philosophy_reason',4,'evaluating freedom through a specified account','An argument must identify whether it concerns non-interference, self-direction, effective capacity or another account. The evaluation depends on that definition.',('A person faces no legal ban but lacks accessible transport. Explain a freedom concern.','Legal non-interference may coexist with limited effective capacity to act. Different theories weigh enabling conditions differently.'),('Using the word freedom means two arguments use the same definition.','False. Clarify the account and its implications before comparing conclusions.'))
concept('philosophy_reason',6,'reconstructing an implicit normative premise','A political argument often relies on an unstated value claim connecting facts to what should be done. Reconstruct it charitably and assess it.',('A policy is cheaper, therefore we ought to adopt it. Identify a missing premise.','The reasoning needs a value premise giving cost decisive or sufficient weight relative to other criteria. That premise can be challenged where fairness or safety matters.'),('Adding factual evidence removes every need for a normative premise.','False. Facts inform the argument but do not alone establish the evaluative standard connecting them to an ought-conclusion.'))
trio('maths_specialist',2,[('Prove sin²x + cos²x = 1 using a unit-circle interpretation.','A point on the unit circle is (cos x, sin x). Its squared distance from the origin is 1, so cos²x + sin²x = 1.'),('Use an identity to simplify 1 − 2sin²x.','From cos 2x = cos²x − sin²x and cos²x = 1 − sin²x, the expression equals cos 2x.'),('Explain why verifying a trigonometric identity for one angle is not a proof for all valid angles.','A single substitution tests one case. A proof uses identities or a general geometric or algebraic argument across the stated domain.')],variant='trig-')
